# ACTIONS
- Start recording activity.
- Stop recording activity.
- List all recorded activity logs.
- List all active (in-progress) activities.
- View the detail of a specific activity log.
- View the detail of a specific active activity.
- Cancel an active (in-progress) activity.
- Delete a recorded activity log.
- Display commands documentation i.e. help.

# COMMANDS

## alog start ACTIVITY_NAME
Start recording an activity.
Error if ACTIVITY_NAME is an active activity.

## alog stop ACTIVITY_NAME [-m "MESSAGE"]
Stop recording an activity.
Error if ACTIVITY_NAME is not an active activity.
Allow user to add the optional argument `-m` to record an activity log message.
If `-m` isn't given, then user is taken to an editor (NANO) to input an activity log message.

## alog list [-a] [-r]
List activities.
Default action is to list all recorded activities, which correspond with the optional argument `-r`.
If `-a` is given, then list the active activities.
If both optional arguments are given, then list activities in both groups.

Example of output:
```
[RECORDED]
project-1
project-2
[ACTIVE]
project-2
project-3
project-4
```
Note that `project-2` appears in both groups because the user has previously recorded a log of the `project-2`
activity, and is currently recording another log of the activity.

## alog info ACTIVITY_NAME [-a] [-r] [-s] 
Display ACTIVITY_NAME activity info.
Default action is to display both the recorded and active activity info, 
which correspond with the optional argument `-ra`. In the scenario that
only "-s" is provided, then the effective option is "-ars".
If `-a` is given, then the info of the active activity is shown. This include the starting datetime of the
activity log, and the running elapsed time so-far.
If both optional arguments are given, then display info of both active and recorded activity.
If `-s` (silence) option is provided, then silent log message and don't print it.

Example of -ar output:
```
[RECORDED]
Sun Apr 26 10:30:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 0 hours 30 minutes 24 seconds

Did an initial commit of the project.

==========
Fri May 1 09:00:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 1 hours 29 minutes 0 seconds

Quick catchup with the team to realign on feature #67.

==========
[ACTIVE]
Fri May 1 11:22:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 3 hours 1 minutes 2 seconds
```

Example of -ars output:
```
[RECORDED]
Sun Apr 26 10:30:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 0 hours 30 minutes 24 seconds
==========
Fri May 1 09:00:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 1 hours 29 minutes 0 seconds
==========
[ACTIVE]
Fri May 1 11:22:00 2026
Elapsed Time: 3 hours 1 minutes 2 seconds
```

## alog cancel ACTIVITY_NAME
Cancel an active activity.
Error if ACTIVITY_NAME isn't an active activity.

## alog delete ACTIVITY_NAME
Delete a recorded activity log.
Error if ACTIVITY_NAME isn't a recorded activity.
Before the activity log is permanently deleted, the user is prompted a confirmation message on the delete.

## alog help
Display alog commands manual.
Help text:
```
Usage: alog command
 alog start activity_name
 alog stop activity_name [-m log_message]
 alog list [-a] [-r]

Commands:
 start activity_name
     Start logging an activity. A timer is started when this command
     is executed, and it'll be stopped when the "stop" command is executed.

     An activity that has started, but not stopped is an active activity.

     When this command is executed and the provided activity_name is an
     active activity, then it'll result in an error.

 stop activity_name [-m log_message]
     Stop logging an active activity. Stops the timer that was started
     when the "start" command was executed, and calculate the elapsed time
     of the activity.

     If the "-m" option was provided, then log_message is recorded into the
     activity log file. Otherwise, the Nano editor is opened to interactively
     record the log message.

     An activity that has been logged is a recorded activity.

     New activity logs are appended to the end of the activity log file.
     See the "info" command.

     When this command is executed and the provided activity_name isn't
     an active activity, then it'll result in an error.

 list [-a] [-r]
     List the user's activities.

     If option "-r" is provided, then recorded activities will be listed.

     If option "-a" is provided, then active activities will be listed.

     The default behaviour, if no options are provided, is to list only
     the recorded activities.

 help
     Print program help text.

 info [-a] [-r]
     TBC
```

# FLOWS
1. start -> list -a -> info -a -> stop
2. start -> list -a -> cancel
3. list -r -> info -r -> delete
4. help
